WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.850
If there's one thing that sets Mark Ruffalo
apart from Bruce Banner, it is his propensity

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:05.620
for spoilers.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.740
Dude means well, but in his enthusiasm for
the MCU, he tends to give the game away.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.440
And we're not talking about a one or two-time
thing it's a bona fide pattern.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.680
Let's start with the undisputable king of
this list: the time Mark Ruffalo spoiled the

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.220
shocking ending of Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.680
We're not talking about something so minor
as, "Oh, Red Skull comes back," or even, "Hey

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.500
guys, Thor crafts a new hammer in an awesome
space forge run by Game of Thrones' Peter

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.200
Dinklage."

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.960
No, Ruffalo did not spoil by halves.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.420
He gave away the astonishing fact of Thanos'
slaughter of half of all life in the universe.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.120
He let loose, in fact, the spoiler equivalent
of the infamous snap itself.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.060
It happened during a Good Morning America
interview alongside Don Cheadle.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.980
At first, he merely approached the spoiler,
teasing, when asked if Thor and the Hulk save

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.820
Asgard in Thor: Ragnarok, that…

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:54.760
"let me just say this..."

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.820
" … like every other Marvel movie, it doesn't
end well for the superheroes."

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.660
He could have stopped there and still done
damage.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.860
But instead, he followed it up with an absolute
firecracker of a sentence:

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.920
"Wait until you see the next one.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.120
Ha — everybody dies!"

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:11.620
"Dude!

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.040
Dude… dude…."

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.000
Ruffalo went on to ask for a rewind, but the
internet never forgets.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.240
Every other moment on this list happened within
the context of an interview, and came from

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.320
Ruffalo's own lips.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.979
It's the easiest way to spoil something, after
all you talk to someone, you're excited to

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.300
promote your work, the whole world is waiting
to see what happens next.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.260
Sometimes you say more than you should.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.640
The stakes are higher when you play one of
the most iconic characters of an already legendary

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.060
film franchise, of course, but the general
principle remains.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.360
From war to friendships, loose talk is so
dangerous because it's so easy.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.460
That's what makes this spoiler so remarkable
it wasn't a slip of the tongue, but a slip

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:50.460
of technology.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.720
"Look at how beautiful Cate is."

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.979
"How big are they gonna cheer for you, Mark?"

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.740
"The camera — the camera loves you."

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.680
"Oh, yeah it does… haha."

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.579
In October 2017, Ruffalo turned on Instagram's
livestream function during the Thor: Ragnarok

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.020
premiere to include fans in the real-time
excitement.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.360
What a cool way to appreciate those fans so
committed to the franchise that they tune

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.760
in for premieres, right?

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:16.200
Unfortunately, he then put his phone in his
pocket and forgot to close the app, ultimately

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.320
streaming the audio of a good portion of the
movie's early scenes.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.200
Happily, Ragnarok's spectacular visuals still
enticed anyone who might have caught the broadcast

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.600
to come see the real thing, but it remains
one heck of a blunder.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.310
Let's be real, though haven't we all left
an app or two running before?

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.269
This is one spoiler that's hard not to forgive.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.760
The entire world held its breath as Avengers:
Endgame approached.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.780
Billboards heralded its arrival in every major
city.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.960
Everyone has a friend or two who spent the
previous month revisiting the decade-spanning

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.200
slate of Marvel movies.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:58.940
Fans congregated online to discuss the odds
of Thanos' defeat, the return of beloved characters,

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.020
and whether or not Cap would finally call
the Avengers to assemble.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.820
As it racked up millions by the second during
its stay in theaters, one thing was made very

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:11.040
clear: every single thing that happens in
Endgame matters to someone.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.580
There is no such thing as a small spoiler
within the context of this kind of culture-defining

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.100
phenomenon.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.020
So Ruffalo revealing the endpoint of a long-developed
relationship at the core of the series is

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.240
kind of a big deal.

00:03:22.629 --> 00:03:26.540
On April 8th, Ruffalo jokingly referred to
the fact that he shot multiple different endings

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.460
to avoid being able to spoil any one aspect
of the film during an interview with E! News.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.040
But he didn't stop there.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.020
He proceeded to point to Chris Evans and say
…

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.680
"He gets married in this!"

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:44.180
As we all know now though Ruffalo apparently
did not at the time that is, in fact, Cap's

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.580
ultimate fate and the final scene of the film.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.400
Evans put it best in his response to this
bombshell:

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.880
"You, like, leak like a bodily function."

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.920
Compared to those weapons-grade spoilers,
this one is rather tame.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:01.800
Or rather, it would be if the MCU weren't
the most emotionally loaded, financially huge,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.180
and imagination-consuming game in town.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.739
Luckily, this little moment of indiscretion
ended up obscured by time and the split nature

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.780
of the final two Thanos-centric movies.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:16.220
In October 2017, Ruffalo mentioned filming
scenes with Rocket Raccoon in an interview

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.340
with USA Today.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:18.180
He said:

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.660
"It's a very funny relationship that the two
of them have.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.740
First of all, it's the biggest superhero and
the smallest.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.240
Just keeping them in a frame together is a
feat and hilarious in itself."

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:28.420
True.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.711
But after Infinity War dawned and no scenes
between the Hulk and Rocket took place, this

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.220
detail became a spoiler.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.260
Suddenly, we knew that Hulk and Rocket were
going to team up in an effort to take Thanos

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:42.480
down, and moreover, that Banner would be able
to access the dormant giant within himself

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.710
anew after struggling to do so during Infinity
War.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:48.300
A retroactive spoiler, but a spoiler regardless.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:54.220
OK, so this next one isn't really a spoiler
it's just silly dudes doing silly things.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.700
Ruffalo's let the flerken out of the bag so
many times now that one can only guess at

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.620
the seriousness of the tongue-lashings he's
probably gotten at Marvel's hands.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:06.700
One moment, however, just might have been
coordinated by the studio itself as a way

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.540
to poke fun at Ruffalo's affinity for spoilage.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:14.240
Ruffalo appeared on The Tonight Show with
Jimmy Fallon in October 2018, where he and

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.120
Fallon riffed on his well-established nature
as a blabbermouth.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.560
"I've been sort of put under surveillance
by Marvel."

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.560
"Marvel, yeah, they don't want you talking
about anything."

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:28.060
Both hyped the segment up as containing a
major spoiler on Twitter, and after it aired,

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.700
directors Joe and Anthony Russo jokingly told
Ruffalo he was fired.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:33.460
So what was the big reveal?

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:36.160
Well… it wasn't really a reveal at all,
actually.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:40.240
Ruffalo seemed to utter the title, but The
Tonight Show bleeped it out, to the chagrin

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:41.480
of fans everywhere.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:47.460
"The title of the next Avengers movie is:
BLEEEEEEEP."

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:52.920
Online sleuths set to decoding the audio and
trying to lip-read, but the results were inconclusive

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.060
until Marvel formally announced it to be Avengers:
Endgame.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.440
So was it a real slip of the tongue?

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.660
Nah this one seemed a bit too self-aware.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:05.550
But now that Ruffalo knows how firmly MCU's
superfans hang onto his every word, maybe

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:09.420
he'll chill out with the spoilers from here
on out… but probably not.

